Release 1 Architecture – Production Foundation

Overview

The Release 1 architecture establishes the initial production foundation for CTB by enabling AI-assisted document reauthoring and validation within the authorised CIB GCP platform boundary.

This release focuses on the minimum set of capabilities required to transition the current PoC into a production-aligned solution. The design intentionally simplifies the architecture by removing advanced orchestration and knowledge platform components that are not required for the first release.

The Release 1 solution is built on a layered pattern:

User Interaction → Application Backend → Document Processing → Platform Shared Services → Storage & Metadata

The architecture is designed to be stable, productionisable, and aligned with enterprise AI and cloud platform standards, while also establishing the foundation for future expansion.

⸻

Release 1 End-to-End Flow
	1.	Document Upload and User Interaction
Documents are uploaded through the CTB Workbench UI, which provides the user-facing interface for document submission, display of reauthored content, validation outputs, and SME review.
	2.	Application Backend Processing
The CTB Processing API Service acts as the central backend service for Release 1. It receives requests from the workbench, coordinates document processing, triggers the AI pipelines, and manages persistence of outputs and metadata.
	3.	Document Processing
Input documents are passed through a deterministic document preprocessing stage to convert raw files into a normalized structure. A subsequent chunking step segments the content into logical sections suitable for downstream AI processing.
	4.	Reauthoring and Validation Pipelines
The reauthor pipeline applies LLM-powered processing to rewrite policy and procedure content into the required target format. The validation pipeline then compares the generated output with the source content to identify potential information gaps, loss, or inconsistencies.
	5.	Enterprise AI Platform Integration
AI processing is integrated with the enterprise Group AI platform through UDI shared services, ensuring that model access, authentication, caching, and other platform capabilities remain aligned with enterprise standards.
	6.	Storage and Metadata Persistence
Raw documents, processed outputs, and validation artefacts are persisted in GCS, while operational processing metadata is captured in BigQuery to support traceability, operational monitoring, and future analytics.
	7.	Human Review and Approval
Processed outputs are presented back to users through the workbench interface for SME review and approval. This provides a human control point before any future downstream knowledge processing is introduced.

⸻

Release 1 Governance and Control Points
	•	Enterprise AI access through UDI shared services
All LLM interactions are routed through the approved enterprise AI integration path, ensuring model access, governance, and monitoring align with organisation-wide standards.
	•	Controlled storage and metadata persistence
Outputs are persisted in platform-managed GCS and BigQuery services rather than local file systems, improving auditability and production readiness.
	•	Human-in-the-loop validation
SME review remains a core control point in Release 1 to ensure generated outputs are reviewed before adoption.

⸻

Release 1 Design Principles
	•	Production-first simplification
The Release 1 design deliberately minimizes architectural complexity to support a stable first production deployment.
	•	Clear separation of application, processing, and platform capabilities
The architecture separates UI/backend responsibilities from document processing and platform shared services.
	•	Enterprise AI alignment
Model access is integrated through the approved enterprise AI platform rather than direct unmanaged model invocation.
	•	Foundation for future evolution
The Release 1 structure is intentionally designed so that additional orchestration and knowledge capabilities can be introduced in later phases without major redesign.

