Release 2 Architecture – Knowledge Platform Expansion

Overview

Release 2 extends CTB from a production document-processing service into a broader knowledge platform architecture.

This phase introduces the architectural components required to support stateful workflow orchestration, governed platform access, ontology-aligned knowledge extraction, vector embeddings, and hybrid retrieval.

The Release 2 architecture is built on a layered pattern:

Ingestion → Canonicalisation → Chunking → Workflow Orchestration → AI Workflows → Governance & Integration → Knowledge Stores → Access

This architecture is designed to support more advanced agent workflows and enterprise knowledge use cases across policy and procedure content.

⸻

Release 2 End-to-End Flow
	1.	Ingestion
Policy and procedure content is onboarded from approved input sources and persisted in the governed platform storage layer. Documents are versioned and registered with operational metadata.
	2.	Preprocessing and Canonicalisation
Raw documents are converted into a canonical chapter/section representation to standardise downstream processing and preserve lineage between source and generated artefacts.
	3.	Chunking
Canonical content is segmented into chunks suitable for downstream embedding generation, knowledge extraction, and retrieval use cases.
	4.	Stateful Workflow Orchestration
Workflow execution is coordinated through LangGraph, enabling stateful execution, conditional branching, retries, and support for more advanced agent workflows.
	5.	Reauthoring and Validation Workflows
Reauthor and validation workflows become independently orchestrated agent pipelines capable of operating within a broader event-driven architecture.
	6.	Knowledge Graph and Embedding Workflows
Once approved, the platform triggers:
	•	Knowledge graph generation, which extracts ontology-aligned artefacts and loads them into Neo4j
	•	Embedding generation, which produces vectors and persists them into the selected enterprise vector capability
	7.	Governed Access via MCP
Platform interaction with storage, metadata, graph, and vector capabilities is mediated through the MCP gateway, providing controlled and auditable access to shared services.
	8.	Retrieval and Access Layer
A query/retrieval API enables hybrid access patterns across graph and vector representations to support future workbench and retrieval use cases.

⸻

Release 2 Governance and Control Points
	•	LangGraph orchestration for stateful AI workflows
Provides deterministic orchestration for more advanced multi-stage agent workflows.
	•	MCP gateway for governed service access
Ensures consistent control over data access, metadata updates, and downstream platform interactions.
	•	Ontology-governed knowledge modelling
Ensures structured knowledge generation remains aligned with the enterprise domain model.
	•	Metadata, lineage, and observability
Supports explainability, traceability, and operational insight across the end-to-end processing lifecycle.

⸻

Release 2 Design Principles
	•	Separation of concerns
Orchestration, model execution, governed I/O, and knowledge persistence are clearly separated.
	•	Stateful workflow enablement
Agent workflows are designed to evolve beyond simple deterministic service execution into reusable stateful orchestration patterns.
	•	Knowledge platform alignment
Graph and vector layers are built as complementary capabilities to support future hybrid retrieval.
	•	Governed extensibility
The architecture supports future AI-driven automation while preserving enterprise control points.

⸻

Release 2 Architecture Design Considerations

1. Event-Driven Workflow Orchestration

The solution adopts an event-driven workflow model to decouple pipeline stages and support asynchronous execution.

Rationale:
This improves resilience, modularity, and scalability as the platform evolves.

⸻

2. Stateful Orchestration with LangGraph

LangGraph is introduced to manage complex workflow coordination, state handling, and future multi-agent expansion.

Rationale:
As the solution moves beyond a deterministic document processing pipeline, stateful orchestration becomes necessary for maintainability and controlled workflow evolution.

⸻

3. Knowledge Graph and Vector Separation

Knowledge graph and vector embeddings are treated as distinct but complementary knowledge representations.

Rationale:
This supports both structured reasoning and semantic retrieval while allowing each capability to evolve independently.

⸻

4. Snapshot-Aligned Knowledge Builds

Knowledge graph and vector outputs are aligned through shared versioning and snapshot identifiers.

Rationale:
This ensures retrieval remains consistent across multiple representations of the same approved document set.

⸻

5. Controlled Access Through MCP

All downstream data and platform interactions are routed through a governed integration layer.

Rationale:
This ensures security, auditability, metadata consistency, and separation between workflow logic and platform access logic.

⸻

6. Ontology Governance

Ontology artefacts remain the canonical knowledge model guiding entity and relationship generation.

Rationale:
This ensures knowledge extraction remains governed and extensible across future use cases.

⸻

Release 2 Summary

Release 2 introduces the broader CTB knowledge platform architecture, enabling stateful AI workflow orchestration, governed platform integration, structured knowledge generation, and semantic retrieval. This phase transforms CTB from a production document-processing solution into a scalable enterprise knowledge platform.

