Overall Summary

The CTB architecture is now intentionally structured as a two-phase platform evolution:
	•	Release 1 productionises the core reauthoring and validation capability on GCP using a simplified application and processing architecture.
	•	Release 2 introduces the full target-state platform architecture required for knowledge graph generation, vector embeddings, governed integration, and future AI-driven knowledge services.

This phased architecture ensures the programme can deliver immediate business value while preserving a clear path to the broader CTB knowledge platform vision.


1. Structural Normalisation (Canonicalisation)

Converting unstructured or semi-structured documents into a consistent canonical representation (e.g., structured markdown or section-based format) where document structure cannot be reliably derived using deterministic parsing alone.

⸻

2. Semantic Segmentation / Chunking

Identifying logical content boundaries based on meaning rather than fixed rules, particularly for complex or inconsistently structured documents.

⸻

3. Content Classification and Tagging

Assigning document types, categories, or business metadata based on contextual understanding of content.

⸻

4. Content Transformation and Standardisation

Applying semantic transformations such as summarisation, rewriting, simplification, or normalisation into a consistent target format or style.

⸻

5. Entity and Relationship Extraction

Extracting structured artefacts (e.g., entities, obligations, controls, relationships) from unstructured text, often aligned to an ontology or business model.

⸻

6. Validation and Semantic Comparison

Comparing source and generated content to detect omissions, inconsistencies, or gaps based on meaning rather than exact text matching.

⸻

7. Embedding Generation for Semantic Retrieval

Generating vector representations of document content using embedding models to enable semantic search, similarity matching, and retrieval use cases.
This capability is distinct from generative LLM usage and is optimised for vectorisation rather than text generation.



