	8. Baseline Observations from Current Reference Use Cases

The current reference use cases indicate that multiple unstructured and semi-structured data patterns are already emerging across the bank. These patterns are valuable because they provide practical implementation examples, but they have evolved in a use-case-led manner rather than through a single strategic architecture.

Key baseline observations are as follows:
	•	Document-centric pattern already exists
Codifying the Bank demonstrates a strong document-led architecture pattern, including ingestion, content preparation, chunking, workflow orchestration, knowledge graph generation, embeddings, vector storage, and retrieval.
	•	Audio and conversational-data pattern already exists
Customer Journey / Voice AI demonstrates an audio- and chat-led pattern, including ingestion of call and chat data, landing into cloud storage, transcription, ML-driven transformation, and downstream serving through structured data platforms and APIs.
	•	Knowledge management and retrieval pattern already exists
The Knowledge Management System demonstrates a managed-content pattern, including authenticated access, content management services, blob and metadata storage, vector search, caching, and AI guardrails.
	•	Common capability themes are visible across the use cases
Across the reference implementations, there is already evidence of common needs around ingestion, transformation, storage, metadata handling, retrieval, access control, and governance.
	•	Current approaches are fragmented
Although relevant capabilities already exist, they are implemented differently across projects, platforms, and storage models. This indicates duplication of effort, differing patterns, and a lack of a shared enterprise approach for unstructured data.
	•	Standardisation opportunity is high
The baseline suggests strong opportunity to define common patterns for source onboarding, content preparation, metadata handling, retrieval, vector and graph usage, and governance controls.

These baseline observations confirm that the workstream is not starting from zero. Instead, it is building on a set of useful but fragmented delivery patterns that now need to be assessed, aligned, and shaped into a more consistent enterprise architecture.

