9. Recommended Position

The recommended immediate position is to proceed with an initial prioritised scope for the Unstructured Data for AI workstream based on two dimensions:
	•	priority content categories, representing the main unstructured and semi-structured data types required by the AI use cases
	•	representative source platforms or systems of record, where these are already known or can be reasonably inferred

Recommended priority content categories

The initial priority content categories are:
	•	documents
	•	audio and transcripts
	•	chat and conversational data
	•	email and communications
	•	images and scanned content
	•	semi-structured business records, including tickets, cases, and work items

Recommended source platform focus

The initial source platform focus should include:
	•	document and collaboration platforms
	•	service and case management platforms
	•	email platforms
	•	contact centre and chat platforms
	•	content management systems
	•	storage and repository platforms
	•	internal application platforms that generate semi-structured records

Recommended architectural position

The workstream should use this prioritised scope to:
	•	establish the initial source inventory
	•	define the baseline architecture view
	•	support capability prioritisation
	•	inform the reference architecture
	•	identify standardisation opportunities and key gaps

The intent is not to produce a complete source-level inventory in the first iteration. Instead, the recommendation is to start with the most relevant content categories and known source platforms, then progressively refine the detail as use case discovery and stakeholder engagement continue.

This approach provides a practical and architecture-led basis for moving forward quickly, while keeping enough flexibility to refine scope as understanding matures.


The baseline shows that relevant unstructured and semi-structured data capability patterns already exist across the current reference use cases. These include document-centric, audio and conversational-data, and managed-content retrieval patterns, with capabilities already visible across ingestion, transformation, storage, retrieval, metadata handling, access control, and AI guardrails.

At the same time, the current landscape has evolved in a use-case-led manner, with different implementation approaches across platforms and solutions. This indicates that while useful capabilities already exist, they are not yet aligned to a consistent shared model.

The baseline therefore provides the current-state view needed to support subsequent reference architecture development, capability standardisation, and structured gap assessment for the workstream.